en-names>IJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Collins</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Association and linkage of leprosy phenotypes with HLA class II and tumour necrosis factor genes</article-title><source>Genes Immun</source><year>2001</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>196</fpage><lpage>204</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11477474</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R175"><label>175</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname> Pachner</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Delaney</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>O'Neill</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Major</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Inoculation of nonhuman primates with the N40 strain of Borrelia burgdorferi leads to a model of Lyme neuroborreliosis faithful to the human disease</article-title><source>Neurology</source><year>1995</year><volume>45</volume><fpage>165</fpage><lpage>72</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7824109</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R176"><label>176</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname> Chang</surname><given-names>YF</given-names></name><name><surname>Novosel</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Chang</surname><given-names>CF</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Experimental induction of chronic borreliosis in adult dogs exposed to Borrelia burgdorferi-infected ticks and treated with dexamethasone</article-title><source>Am J Vet Res</source><year>2001</year><volume>62</volume><fpage>1104</fpage><lpage>12</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11453487</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. (1)</label><caption><p>The presence of high number of <italic>Treponema pallidum</italic> inlate/chronic neurosyphilis. Numerous spirochetes accumulated in the cerebral cortex of a patient suffering from general paresis. Immunostaining was performed using a polyclonal anti-<italic>Treponemapallidum</italic> antibody (Biodesign, B65210R). Bar corresponds to 20 &#x000b5;m. The illustration was done in relation with a previously published study, where the material and methods used were described in detail [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R34">34</xref>].</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TONEUJ-6-146_F1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. (2)</label><caption><p>Arteritis in meningovascular Lyme neuroborreliosis. The proliferation of the intima is well visible in panel A and arrows in panel Bpoint to lymphocytic infiltrates in the thickened adventitia. Magnification: x 50. The photomicrographs were taken from a previouslypublished study, which reported the detailed clinical and neuropathological findings of this case [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R47">47</xref>]. Compare panels A and B of this figurewith those illustrated for Heubner&#x02019;s arteritis in neurosyphilis (Greenfield&#x02019;s Neuropathology, Figs 6 and 8, page 257 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R25">25</xref>]).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TONEUJ-6-146_F2"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. (3)</label><caption><p><italic>B. burgdorferi</italic> cultivated and detected in the brain in the atrophic form of Lyme neuroborreliosis. Spirochetes cultivated (<bold>A</bold>) anddetected by silver-impregnation technique (<bold>B</bold>) in the brain of a patient with slowly progressive dementia who suffered from Lyme neuroborreliosis. Bars A: 20 &#x000b5;m; B: 30 &#x000b5;m. The illustrations were taken from previous studies, which were the subject of severalpublications [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R34">34</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R68">68</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R99">99</xref>]. Compare the distribution of <italic>B. burgdorferi</italic> in B with that of <italic>T. pallidum</italic> in general paresis in Fig. (<bold><xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">1</xref></bold>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TONEUJ-6-146_F3"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Pathological Manifestations of Neurosyphilis</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="cols" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Stages</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Types</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pathology</th></tr><tr><th colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">PrimaryStage</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">SkinManifestation (Chancre)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">LocalInflammation</td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Secondarystage or</td><td rowspan="4" colspan="1">Meningeallues</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meningitis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="3" colspan="1"><bold>Earlyneurosyphilis</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Vasculitis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Neuritis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mixed forms</td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tertiarystage or </td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meningovascular syphilis</td><td rowspan="2" colspan="1">Meningealvasculitis with secondary cerebral infarcts </td></tr><tr><td rowspan="3" colspan="1"><bold>Late/chronic neurosyphilis</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Generalparesis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tabesdorsalis</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Chronicmeningoencephalitis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mixed forms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">(Infiltrative and atrophic form)</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2.</label><caption><p>Pathological Manifestations of Lyme Neuroborreliosis</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="cols" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Stages</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Types</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pathology</th></tr><tr><th colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Primarystage</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Erythemamigrans</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Localinflammation</td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Secondarystage</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meningeal involvement</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meningitis, vasculitis</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>EarlyLyme neuroborreliosis </bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cranial andperipheral nerves Mixed forms</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Neuritis</td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tertiarystage</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meningovascular Lyme neuroborreliosis</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meningitis &#x00026;occlusive vasculitis, cerebral infarcts</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Late or chronic Lyme neuroborreliosis</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lyme meningoencephalitis or meningoencephalomyelitis</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Infiltrative&#x00026; atrophic forms of meningoencephalitis</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></floats-group></article>